\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
Uvbie (also Uvwie, Uvwi\d{e}; ISO 639-3 \texttt{evh}, Glottocode uvbi1238) is spoken by the Uvwie people in Delta State, Nigeria, with a reported (dated) figure of about 20,000 speakers. Very little exists; no school materials were found.
\section{Primary Educational Resources}
None found in search results.
\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item University of Benin repository: ``A Proposed Orthography of Uvwi\d{e}'' (undergraduate thesis, 2015, by Efeturi Evuarherhe), proposing a writing system from native-speaker SIL wordlist data. \url{https://repository.uniben.edu/taxonomy/term/2261}
\item Related University of Benin repository PDF (Efeturi). \url{https://repository.uniben.edu/sites/default/files/2026-02/EFETURI.pdf}
\item Wikipedia, Uvbie language. \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uvbie_language}
\end{itemize}
\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Joshua Project profile; states that local-language Scripture access is limited. No completed Uvbie Bible translation was found. \url{https://www.joshuaproject.net/index.php/people_groups/15753}
\end{itemize}
\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
None found.
\section{Curriculum Development Context}
The language is close to Urhobo, for which more material exists, but that was not verified here and Urhobo resources are not listed as Uvbie resources. A curriculum would start from orthography development.
\end{document}
