\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
Evenki (ISO 639-3 \texttt{evn}, Glottocode even1259) is an endangered Tungusic language spoken in Russia, China and Mongolia. Searches found grammars, orthography descriptions and general references, but no current school textbook listings.
\section{Primary Educational Resources}
Search results mention Soviet-era readers (e.g. a first reader on the Tungus language compiled by Vasilevich, Latin script from 1931, Cyrillic from 1937) and school teaching reportedly since 1980, but no specific current textbook with a URL was found.
\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia, Evenki orthography (Russian Cyrillic, Chinese Latin-based and Mongolian-based systems). \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evenki_orthography}
\item Wikipedia, Evenki grammar. \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evenki_grammar}
\item Nedjalkov, \emph{Evenki} (Routledge, 1997), a comprehensive grammar. \url{https://booktopia.com.au/evenki-igor-nedjalkov/book/9780415026406.html}
\end{itemize}
\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None identified.
\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Omniglot, Evenki writing. \url{https://mail.omniglot.com/writing/evenki.htm}
\end{itemize}
\section{Curriculum Development Context}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia, Evenki language. \url{https://wikipedia.com/wiki/ISO_639:evn}
\item Arctic Megapedia, Evenk language. \url{https://arctic-megapedia.com/en/?p=2658}
\end{itemize}
Script choice differs by country, which a curriculum must address.
\end{document}
